
<p>1Password:</p>

<blockquote>
  <ul>
<li><p>An attacker gained access to some of our backend systems and
copied a limited amount of customer account information from a
subset of our customers, including the names and email addresses
that customers use to sign into 1Password.</p></li>
<li><p><em>Your vault data, the items in them, and the keys that protect
them are safe and were not affected.</em> 1Password does not have
access to your password or secret keys.</p></li>
<li><p>We implemented containment measures, restored clean versions of
the affected service, and have triggered a rotation of internal
credentials.</p></li>
<li><p>You do not need to update anything in your 1Password account.</p></li>
<li><p>We recommend being cautious of phishing attempts in the form of
unexpected emails about 1Password, such as invitations or
account recovery requests that you did not initiate.</p></li>
<li><p>1Password apps and services are working normally.</p></li>
</ul>
</blockquote>

<p>It seems like they dealt with this perfectly. By design, the sensitive data customers store with 1Password <em>can’t</em> be accessed without the secret keys that only customers possess. This is why end-to-end encryption is essential.</p>

